{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, took a burnt-offering and sacrifices for Elohim.</b> In all the sacrifices we do not find the name Elohim but rather the specific Name (YHWH); but Jethro did not yet know YHWH, so he offered to Elohim. And this is why it says of Moses: ״את כל אשר עשה ה׳ לפרעה״ — all that YHWH did to Pharaoh — whereas of Jethro it mentions: ״את כל אשר עשה אלהים למשה״ — all that Elohim did to Moses." "<b>With Moses' father-in-law, before the Elohim.</b> Before the dwelling-place of the Elohim, which is the pillar of cloud and the pillar of fire, where the dwelling of the Elohim was. And this feast they made in honor of Jethro, who converted by circumcision and immersion in water, according to the law of the convert who comes to take shelter under the wings of the Shekhinah. And there is no doubt that this feast for Aaron and the seventy elders, and the feast of delicacies for Isaac our father — their intent is one: that joy may come upon the soul of the eater and the holy spirit (רוה\"ק) may rest upon him, for the powers of the soul are bound to the powers of the body, and when the powers of the body are aroused, the powers of the soul are strengthened toward whatever matter the eater intends. And this is the meaning of the harp among the prophets who come to arouse upon themselves a spirit from on high, in the manner of what is written (II Kings 3:15): ״והיה כנגן המנגן ותהי עליו יד ה׳״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {1 "רוה\"ק resolved as רוח הקודש (holy spirit). The reference to 'the feast of delicacies for Isaac our father' alludes to Genesis 26:30; translated as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:42.499263942Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}